  "statement": "(i) Show that $x^{4}+x^{2}+1=(x^{2}+x+1)(x^{2}-x+1)$. (ii) Deduce that $x^{6}-1=(x-1)(x+1)(x^{2}+x+1)(x^{2}-x+1)$. (iii) Hence write down all six complex sixth roots of unity.",
  "solution": "(i) $(x^{2}+1)^{2}-x^{2}=(x^{2}+1-x)(x^{2}+1+x)$. (ii) $x^{6}-1=(x^{3}-1)(x^{3}+1)=(x-1)(x^{2}+x+1)(x+1)(x^{2}-x+1)$, which by (i) equals $(x^{3}-1)(x^{3}+1)$; grouping gives the stated four factors. (iii) Roots: $1,-1,(-1\\\\pm i\\\\sqrt{3})/2,(1\\\\pm i\\\\sqrt{3})/2$.",
